    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1008">

                <pb facs="0881" n="765" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg765"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.113/236.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Mexican Ambassador</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 137.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-03-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 26, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your excellency’s note No. 1170, of the 21st instant and of your
                    excellency’s telegrams of the 21st, 22d, and 23d,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1008-f1"
                    /> respectively, relative to the matter of excepting from the operation of the
                    President’s proclamation of the 14th instant certain shipments of arms and
                    munitions of war destined for the use of your excellency’s Government.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to inform you that the President has this day decided to except
                    the shipments in question from the operation of the proclamation mentioned, and
                    has ordered released for exportation <pb facs="0882" n="766" xml:id="pg766"/>
                    into Mexico the arms and munitions of war specifically mentioned in your note
                    No. 1170 under reference.</p>
                <p>In this connection I also have the honor to say to your excellency that whenever
                    from now on it is represented to the Department that specific consignments of
                    arms and ammunition are intended for the use of your excellency’s Government,
                    the consignor will be informed that he should take the matter up with the
                    Embassy under your excellency’s worthy charge, and that upon submittal by the
                    Embassy to the Department of the necessary particulars relative to the
                    consignments the matter will be given prompt attention and placed before the
                    President as soon as possible for his final decision as to whether or not they
                    may be exported.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Huntington Wilson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
